  "question": {
    "text": "In which one of the following metal carbonyls, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math> forms a bridge between metal atoms?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "xRyN1",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "vMA8i",
      "text": " <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Mn</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "x0XA2",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Os</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hkrEW",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><msub><mi>Ru</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>CO</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In the most common bridging mode, denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> or simply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math>  ligand bridges a pair of metals. This bonding mode is observed in the commonly available metal carbonyls: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>8</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>9</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>12</mn></msub></math>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Co</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mfenced><mi>CO</mi></mfenced><mn>12</mn></msub></math>. In certain higher nuclearity clusters, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi></math> bridges between three or even four metals.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/472f7b34-dc0c-4cf6-9d3d-810d2c51c0ea.png\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 345px;\"/></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/6d401b5e-dfb5-46e2-9df8-9aada139e331.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 526px;\"/></p>",
